  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Tasks, contact groups, and contacts, calendar items and tasks in Deleted Items and Recoverable Items can be reached by their ids in every mailbox. In most mailboxes Microsoft Graph names the default folders by a compressed id that an item's own id does not give, and such calls failed. Changes that go through the mailbox's export and import, such as SuppressReadReceipts and MarkAllItemsAsRead, work on long messages: an export over 32 KB comes in several records, and such items failed with ErrorInternalServerError. The default calendar's and contacts folder's ids are written as Exchange writes them, the same as ParentFolderId of the items in them. GetInboxRules numbers Priority from 1, as EWS does, in mailboxes where Microsoft Graph's sequence does not. A deleted item in a mailbox with compressed folder ids is reported as a MovedEvent into Recoverable Items, not as a DeletedEvent. AddDelegate of the mailbox owner gives ErrorDelegateCannotAddOwner. On netstandard2.0 the package depends on System.Security.Cryptography.Xml 10.0.14, which closes three denial-of-service advisories. Tasks use the extended properties Microsoft To Do now keeps. With Tasks.ReadWrite.All alone a task's size, percent complete, work and billing fields, sensitivity, culture, HTML body and single-value extended properties are read and written; only Companies, Contacts, a regenerating recurrence and array extended properties still need the mailbox permissions. A new task no longer names the mailbox as its Owner, as in Exchange. FindItem and the first SyncFolderItems list tasks of a class of their own (IPM.Task.Something), with their class, when the application has MailboxItem.Read.All. Task folders take single-value extended properties in CreateFolder, UpdateFolder, GetFolder and FindFolder. SoftDelete and HardDelete of contacts and contact groups, and EmptyFolder on folders that hold them, use the mailbox's own delete when the application has the optional MailboxItem.ReadWrite.All permission, in one call and as EWS does them; without it they work as before.",
